decisions:
  - id: skills-north-star-model
    decision: 'North star is a plain-language goal: field plus review: auto|manual|off. Not an assertion framework.'
    rationale: 'User clarified: the point is a blurb to remember the purpose, not a test runner. The user+agent combo evaluates at review time. Simpler, more honest about what automated evaluation can and can''t do.'
  - id: skills-not-lore
    decision: Skills are instructions (closer to protocols) not events (lore). Version history tracks body+goal changes, not an append-only event stream.
    rationale: 'Reviewer blocking finding from round 2: append-only instruction history is redundant with git and inverts the protocol/lore distinction without justification.'
  - id: skill-call-is-assembler
    decision: 'paradigm_skill_call is a prompt assembler: validates inputs, substitutes $ARGUMENTS, returns assembled prompt string. It does not replicate context:fork or agent: routing.'
    rationale: 'Reviewer correctly identified: Claude Code runtime features are not text substitutions. The tool is useful but limited. Document the constraint rather than overpromise.'
  - id: review-mode-field
    decision: 'Skills get a review: auto|manual|off field controlling classification behavior per skill.'
    rationale: 'User explicitly flagged: not all skills should allow agent auto-classification. High-stakes skills (deploys, migrations) need human review on every invocation regardless of how obvious the outcome appears.'
  - id: skills-neverland-colaunch
    decision: Skills system ships with Neverland on May 1 — co-designed, not sequenced after.
    rationale: User stated May 1 hard deadline for true Paradigm + Neverland launch. The architect's wait-for-Neverland argument was immediately invalid.
learnings:
  - The north star framing shifted from an assertion framework to a plain-language goal field — much simpler, more honest, and lets the agent infer clarity naturally from skill type
  - The auto/manual/off review mode respects that different skills carry different stakes — don't assume agent judgment is always appropriate
  - Skills as managed units needs a registry but the registry should be gitignored runtime state — not a new committed artifact
  - The content/creative skill category (format, tone, reference) is genuinely different from the 22 existing dev-tooling orchestration flows — needs separate treatment
  - paradigm_skill_call being a prompt assembler is a constraint to document honestly, not a gap to fill — it still provides value for input validation and discoverability
